    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="9e1df7ff-3bfa-49a3-86c7-fd39a861bd74">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">concept</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="80caaea3-336b-4e6a-a85c-76531d794294">
      <data key="d2">fact_entity</data>
      <data key="d4">concept</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="2cbd5814-f54a-4af1-92c0-18cfd941020d">
      <data key="d3">Because the insurance company offers group coverage with flexible benefit options, recipients may accelerate payouts, thereby decreasing the total death benefit available later.</data>
      <data key="d4">causes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="a4f89de4-1afc-4c97-948f-48c37a6cfc59">
      <data key="d3">The reduction in the death benefit due to advances depends on having flexible coverage options like portability, which enables claimants to access funds before death without requalification.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="89ba6d0e-f1eb-478c-935c-effa8d9500ab">
      <data key="d3">The reduction in the final death benefit occurs because advances are deducted from the overall payout. This limitation applies specifically to those who qualify under conditions like a terminal illness diagnosis with a short life expectancy.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies condition</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="ad33389a-5e72-4baf-8990-51a347fe4a8a">
      <data key="d3">The reduction in the final death benefit occurs because prior advances—such as those made under terminal illness provisions—are subtracted from the original policy's face value.</data>
      <data key="d4">caused by</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="d03f6263-7bbe-40c1-89fa-5e693533134a">
      <data key="d3">Accelerating any portion of the death benefit, regardless of size, directly decreases the total sum available to beneficiaries. Since the smallest allowable advance is $5,000, this sets the baseline for reductions.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="800afedf-6828-402d-ac61-26fa74c9cb5b">
      <data key="d3">Since each accelerated payment reduces the ultimate death benefit, the $250,000 ceiling ensures that even at its highest, the impact on the final payout remains bounded.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="f148ea16-1810-4ee0-8bd3-fc0b480c1222">
      <data key="d3">Since each accelerated payment diminishes the remaining death benefit, the use of an actuarial discount justifies this reduction based on time value and risk factors inherent in early disbursement.</data>
      <data key="d4">causes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="afec02f1-3018-4d35-a72f-90bd5e82289f">
      <data key="d3">Notification by beneficiaries triggers the claims process, enabling potential access to accelerated benefits, which would reduce the ultimate death benefit. Without prompt reporting, the timeline for processing accelerations or payouts may be delayed.</data>
      <data key="d4">precedes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911">
      <data key="d3">Accelerating a portion of the death benefit directly impacts the total amount available for beneficiaries, regardless of how many beneficiaries exist. This effect occurs independently of whether benefits are split equally or designated differently.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="30ed27ef-e97f-4082-96c0-a90281492f8f">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_1 explains how taking an advance affects the ultimate death benefit, while fact_sentence_2 indicates where one can get the necessary claim form. The second fact supports procedural access relevant to initiating a claim, which may lead to acceleration under certain conditions described in the first.</data>
      <data key="d4">precedes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="4a5e1799-2abe-49d2-a8ee-2933001fa99f">
      <data key="d3">The acceleration of benefits impacts the final payout to beneficiaries, which is relevant because such claims must be formally processed through the designated department at Alliance Group Benefits.</data>
      <data key="d4">procedural dependency</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="2a484835-c2d5-4916-aaf4-80a161c5cdca">
      <data key="d3">Accelerating a portion of the death benefit reduces future payments to beneficiaries, which complements the earlier point about converting to an individual policy by showing how financial decisions affect ultimate payout outcomes even after transitioning coverage.</data>
      <data key="d4">complies with</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="8eab46db-8762-4987-ab49-5c7a33da05bf">
      <data key="d3">If someone converts their group coverage into an individual policy after termination, they retain the option to accelerate part of the death benefit should they become terminally ill, resulting in a reduced final payout to beneficiaries.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="84ecaece-d3be-49c1-95f0-cc92e7c46732">
      <data key="d3">Accelerating portions of the death benefit affects the actual amount available during conversion, meaning the $200,000 ceiling might be effectively lowered depending on earlier withdrawals.</data>
      <data key="d4">affected by</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="03d8ddf1-d4d8-4fbc-aa24-7b7db121df87">
      <data key="d3">Portability without proof of insurability preserves access to coverage post-employment, yet advances against the death benefit reduce the final sum paid out, limiting the effectiveness of long-term protection.</data>
      <data key="d4">modifies</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="a53bb99a-3253-4bd8-9b0c-bc4334f7b67d">
      <data key="d3">Accelerated death benefits reduce the final death benefit available upon claim, making timely action—such as porting coverage within 31 days—a critical step to preserve maximum protection.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="bb3b7a5f-abd9-466a-a39b-019177c58922">
      <data key="d3">Accelerated payments from the death benefit, permitted under the policy terms, directly affect the total amount available to beneficiaries, thereby influencing the significance of the coverage confirmed in fact_sentence_1.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="f2fe91f4-2acc-437e-b04b-0b025b2ef31c">
      <data key="d3">The group life insurance policy's provision for reduced payouts due to accelerations relies on the structural foundation established by the employer-administered Master Group Policy.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="668f2149-7691-4670-aa11-f23fbe2f1f88">
      <data key="d3">Rules limiting death benefits due to accelerations rely on higher-level policies, which override individual certificates per clause one.</data>
      <data key="d4">controls</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="70945007-c2b7-452b-9909-9a125e9b73e5">
      <data key="d3">Since the death benefit can be reduced through acceleration, this limitation is consistent with the overarching terms outlined in the Master Policy mentioned in fact_sentence_1, reinforcing its binding nature.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="2c03a154-97c0-4d73-ad37-1599582fab56">
      <data key="d3">Since Jordan Casey Taylor is the primary beneficiary, reductions from accelerated payouts under the policy's terms directly diminish the financial benefit they would otherwise inherit.</data>
      <data key="d4">affects beneficiary</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="c2b096bf-e2af-46ce-9492-7da5270a16a1">
      <data key="d3">The rule that accelerations reduce the death benefit directly affects Jordan Casey Taylor, who is listed as the spouse beneficiary entitled to the payout.</data>
      <data key="d4">affects beneficiary</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="b9fb6a96-3110-4371-bdfa-b3a62b485271">
      <data key="d3">Accelerating part of the death benefit under the policy directly undermines the guarantee of a full 100% payout to the primary beneficiary, limiting the actual amount received.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces payout</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="52b0c634-376a-4bbe-a0e1-f374e691f95a">
      <data key="d3">If the death benefit is partially paid out early due to acceleration, the Taylor Family Revocable Trust's contingent share could be diminished accordingly.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduced by</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144" target="0fd4ce41-df87-49ef-b846-edcb6c8e3ffc">
      <data key="d3">Accelerating part of the death benefit directly impacts the amount ultimately paid to the contingent beneficiary, who would otherwise receive 100%, thus limiting their potential payout based on earlier advances.</data>
      <data key="d4">reduces payout</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
