    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="2cbd5814-f54a-4af1-92c0-18cfd941020d">
      <data key="d3">The possibility of benefits being paid to the estate depends on the prior establishment of group coverage managed by the insurer, ensuring claims processes are defined even in absence of named beneficiaries.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="50012b48-7eab-42a1-9339-4f987a82053c">
      <data key="d3">Even without pre-existing condition limitations, the policy includes safeguards like directing unclaimed benefits to the estate when no beneficiaries survive.</data>
      <data key="d4">contextual relation</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="79accc6d-4303-44fb-afe3-1912cdb727f7">
      <data key="d3">When no beneficiary survives the insured, it underscores why timely updates to beneficiary designations are important, reinforcing the practical necessity of being able to modify them through HR at any point.</data>
      <data key="d4">contingency for</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911">
      <data key="d3">Fact sentence 1 outlines the fallback arrangement when all beneficiaries predecease the insured, ensuring clarity on where funds go if no individual recipient remains. This scenario contrasts with the standard case covered in fact sentence 2, where multiple beneficiaries exist.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="afec02f1-3018-4d35-a72f-90bd5e82289f">
      <data key="d3">The rule about paying benefits to the estate if no beneficiary survives informs the context for why prompt reporting matters, ensuring proper administrative follow-through even in such cases.</data>
      <data key="d4">informs</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="30ed27ef-e97f-4082-96c0-a90281492f8f">
      <data key="d3">If no beneficiary survives the insured, the death benefit is payable to the estate, ensuring funds are distributed even without living beneficiaries. This outcome depends on submitting proper documentation, such as the claim form mentioned in the second sentence.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c" target="6a34626d-60fc-44c8-a650-49df4e862518">
      <data key="d3">The rule about paying benefits to the estate applies when no living beneficiary exists, ensuring funds are distributed appropriately after death. This outcome depends on successfully filing a claim, which requires a certified death certificate as part of the necessary documentation.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
