    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="2cbd5814-f54a-4af1-92c0-18cfd941020d">
      <data key="d3">Beneficiary allocation rules depend on the presence of group coverage established by the insurance provider, which enables defined procedures for claims processing.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="c3c8847b-85e9-4a8d-9b64-6b4b5efd5144">
      <data key="d3">The equal division rule applies only after determining the final death benefit amount, meaning it operates on the residual sum left after any accelerations have been applied.</data>
      <data key="d4">influences</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="79accc6d-4303-44fb-afe3-1912cdb727f7">
      <data key="d3">Knowing that benefits are split equally unless otherwise specified supports the importance of timely beneficiary updates, allowing individuals to customize payouts according to their intentions.</data>
      <data key="d4">informs</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="80ef03d9-a0d8-4e34-b854-917b328e532c">
      <data key="d3">Fact sentence 2 establishes the default rule for dividing benefits among multiple beneficiaries, assuming equal shares unless stated otherwise. This principle becomes relevant only when at least one beneficiary survives, making it contextually dependent on the outcome described in fact sentence 1.</data>
      <data key="d4">conditional on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="6a34626d-60fc-44c8-a650-49df4e862518">
      <data key="d3">The rule about equal division of benefits among multiple beneficiaries applies after a claim has been successfully filed, contingent upon submission of proper documentation such as a certified death certificate.</data>
      <data key="d4">precedes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="fabc920e-4e63-48bd-8ae0-5520935f9ace">
      <data key="d3">The rule about equal division among beneficiaries applies after a claim has been successfully filed and processed. This ensures clarity on how funds are distributed once the necessary paperwork is complete.</data>
      <data key="d4">precedes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.65</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="ba0648f1-21c7-41d2-baec-33f72f4b198e">
      <data key="d3">The rule about equal distribution of benefits among multiple beneficiaries relies on accurate identification to ensure proper allocation. Without confirming who the rightful beneficiaries are, equitable distribution cannot occur.</data>
      <data key="d4">enables</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="afec02f1-3018-4d35-a72f-90bd5e82289f">
      <data key="d3">The equal division of benefits among multiple beneficiaries depends on accurate and prompt reporting by those beneficiaries. Notifying Alliance Group Benefits promptly ensures the claims process begins without delay, allowing equitable disbursement according to policy terms.</data>
      <data key="d4">precedes</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.85</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="bb3b7a5f-abd9-466a-a39b-019177c58922">
      <data key="d3">The rule about equal division of benefits among multiple beneficiaries applies directly to the coverage details provided in fact_sentence_1, ensuring clarity on how the $280,000 benefit would be distributed per the named beneficiaries.</data>
      <data key="d4">depends on</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="f2fe91f4-2acc-437e-b04b-0b025b2ef31c">
      <data key="d3">Benefit distribution rules apply to the group life insurance policy managed by the employer, ensuring clarity in payouts based on the designations made under the master policy.</data>
      <data key="d4">governs</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="70945007-c2b7-452b-9909-9a125e9b73e5">
      <data key="d3">While the first fact emphasizes adherence to policy terms, the second fact adds operational detail about equal distribution among co-beneficiaries, reinforcing how the beneficiary structure outlined in the first summary would function upon claim submission.</data>
      <data key="d4">complements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.92</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="2c03a154-97c0-4d73-ad37-1599582fab56">
      <data key="d3">The general rule about equal distribution among multiple beneficiaries supports the clarity of designation in fact_sentence_1, ensuring consistent application of beneficiary intent.</data>
      <data key="d4">applies to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="c2b096bf-e2af-46ce-9492-7da5270a16a1">
      <data key="d3">The rule stating that benefits are split equally without specification applies in cases where more than one beneficiary exists, which contrasts with the current case where only one primary beneficiary (Taylor) is named.</data>
      <data key="d4">applies to case</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.75</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="b9fb6a96-3110-4371-bdfa-b3a62b485271">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 outlines the default equal-split rule for multiple beneficiaries, which supports the clarity provided in fact_sentence_1 about sole beneficiary entitlement, reinforcing procedural consistency in claim disbursement.</data>
      <data key="d4">corroborates</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="52b0c634-376a-4bbe-a0e1-f374e691f95a">
      <data key="d3">The principle that benefits are split equally among multiple beneficiaries applies directly to scenarios involving contingent designations like the Taylor Family Revocable Trust, ensuring clarity in payout distribution under default conditions.</data>
      <data key="d4">applies to</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.95</data>
    </edge>
    <edge source="6158d9a5-ff94-4835-99aa-58729bb45911" target="0fd4ce41-df87-49ef-b846-edcb6c8e3ffc">
      <data key="d3">Fact_sentence_2 establishes that benefits divide equally among multiple beneficiaries unless stated otherwise, supporting the interpretation that the contingent beneficiary receives 100% only because no co-beneficiary is mentioned alongside them.</data>
      <data key="d4">supplements</data>
      <data key="d2">fact_fact</data>
      <data key="d5">0.9</data>
    </edge>
